{"name":"Amber Sienna","hex":"#c0863f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"amber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ambergris, the waxy product of the sperm whale. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\n\nFormerly thought to be the product of a plant.","Formerly thought to be the product of a plant."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a brownish yellow colour, like that of most amber."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amber"},{"term":"sienna","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A form of clay containing iron and manganese.","A pigment with a reddish-brown colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["having a reddish-brown colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sienna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amber","blocks":[{"text":"Amber is fossilized tree resin. It has been appreciated for its color and natural beauty since Neolithic times, and worked as a gemstone since classical antiquity. Amber is used in jewelry and as a healing agent in folk medicine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Amber2.jpg/330px-Amber2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber","wikidata":"Q25381"},{"title":"Sienna","blocks":[{"text":"Sienna is an earth pigment containing iron oxide and manganese oxide. In its natural state, it is yellowish brown, and it is called raw sienna. When heated, it becomes a reddish brown, and it is called burnt sienna. It takes its name from the city-state of Siena, where it was produced during the Renaissance. Along with ochre and umber, it was one of the first pigments to be used by humans, and is found in many cave paintings. Since the Renaissance, it has been one of the brown pigments most widely used by artists."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sienna","wikidata":"Q899318"},{"title":"Amber (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The color amber is a pure chroma color, located on the color wheel midway between the colors of yellow and orange. The color name is derived from the material also known as amber, which is commonly found in a range of yellow-orange-brown-red colors; likewise, as a color, amber can refer to a range of yellow-orange colors. In English, the first recorded use of the term as a color name, rather than a reference to the specific substance, was in 1500."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Ant_in_amber_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amber_(color)","wikidata":"Q679355"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg/500px-Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg/500px-Amber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Unpolished amber stones","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmber_Bernstein_many_stones.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Amber_Fisher.jpg/500px-Amber_Fisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Amber_Fisher.jpg/500px-Amber_Fisher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fishing for amber on the coast of Baltic Sea, near Gdańsk, Poland. Winter storms throw out amber nuggets.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmber_Fisher.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg/500px-Gouttes-drops-resine-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi